# SFT execution status v0.125: fixed generated-only VM entry connected

2026-09-27. **A concrete two-unit Linux supervisor, fixed cold coordinator
entry, generated-fixture builder and authenticated source bundle are ready
for native qualification.** The local run passed 229 tests with zero failures,
errors or skips. This is not a native pass, a real checkpoint import, or Gate-5
completion. No AWS resource or S3 object was accessed in this step.

## What now connects

`root supervisor -> isolated preparation unit -> stop + empty cgroup ->
read-only source/input binds -> fresh cold import unit -> actual coordinator
and package -> independent checksum consumer -> stop + empty cgroup ->
input/source hash readback -> tmpfs cleanup -> bounded aggregate result`

Preparation creates only the fixed 17-token-vocabulary, one-layer tiny model
and opaque synthetic tokenizer fixtures. The import entry accepts no paths,
model selector, approval flag, or real-data mode. It requires the exact generated
scope/geometry, bound canonical controls and small content caps before input
access. It calls the actual v4 runtime gate before control reads and again
through the coordinator: the native script contains no runtime-check bypass.

The cold import sees worker-owned source files and generated inputs through
read-only mounts. Their writable host aliases sit beneath a root-owned 0700
attempt directory, and the preparation unit must be stopped with an empty
cgroup before import dispatch. The worker has no network, GPU devices or
credentials. The parent checks the staged source against its release manifest
and compares all generated-input/source hashes again after import.

This uses the existing systemd control helpers without changing their code or
globals. Mount composition was checked against the official
[systemd 255 execution specification](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/systemd/systemd/v255/man/systemd.exec.xml).
Its semantics still need observation on the target Linux host; local doubles
are not proof of namespace behavior or external-writer exclusion.

## Bounds and truth boundaries

- Exactly one preparation unit followed by one import unit, never retry after
  start. Each has 2-GiB memory, zero swap, 64 tasks and a 90-second independent
  systemd deadline. The controller has a 240-second cooperative budget; a
  native operator must additionally apply a 300-second outer timeout.
- A new 64-MiB host tmpfs stages generated files. The import unit has another
  64-MiB private work tmpfs; only a <=4-KiB aggregate crosses the result bind.
  These are capacities, not cumulative native-write limits. Python/module
  reads and the extra independent checksum verification are not added to the
  coordinator's content counters.
- The tiny fixture keeps the established 8-MiB DCP execution cap; total
  coordinator reservations are capped at 32 MiB reads / 4 MiB private writes.
  The unchanged general planner has a 16-GiB minimum planning floor even for
  this geometry. The separate 2-GiB tiny rehearsal explicitly does **not**
  establish that full-size resource envelope or host admission.
- Failed cleanup prevents success, retains a fixed cleanup-failure record,
  and requires operator action. No force/lazy unmount occurs while a cgroup
  is nonempty. On successful cleanup generated tmpfs payloads disappear;
  root-owned started/failure/result evidence remains for collection. Billing
  expiry and instance termination belong to the separately admitted operator.

## Verified locally

The final 12-module suite ran in 5.229 seconds on the existing Mac CPython
3.13.7 environment: **229 tests, zero failures/errors/skips**. Twenty-five
tests are new; 204 are related prior regressions. Ruff passed for all eight
new Python files. A parent audit guard recorded zero network and zero real
artifact-access attempts during this suite. Fresh child tests load only the
listed scripts/source; the audit hook is not an OS boundary and does not
automatically propagate to child interpreters.

The genuine local entry tests substitute host observations (runtime gate,
read-only filesystem flags and loader location) only. Actual controls,
source/config hashes, conversion, packaging, two input audits and independent
checksum verification execute. Dispatch/mount/UID tests double every native
operation. No Mac mount, privilege change or heavy checkpoint workload ran.

Separate extracted-bundle verification checked every file hash, created tiny
generated inputs and ran the complete entry under the same local host-only
substitutions. This caught one concrete packaging defect: the source-closure
builder could not discover v3's computed inert-namespace import. Added its
explicit source seed and a regression. The incomplete v0.1 archive remains
preserved; it was never uploaded or executed as a native attempt.

Use only the corrected archive in `artifacts/lumi-vm-coordinator-source-v0.2/`:
